from pathlib import Path
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[3]
sections={
'journal/rental-equipment-maintenance-log/index.html':('service-trigger-review','''<section id="service-trigger-review"><h2>Keep the service trigger, completed work and release separate</h2><p>Record the source of each requirement before choosing a next-due value. A calendar date and a meter threshold can describe different triggers. Where your equipment documentation says either trigger requires action, reaching one is enough to start that action; a later date does not cancel an earlier meter trigger. Do not invent a service interval from the numbers in this guide.</p><p>In the fictional sheet, 520 hours is a recorded target and the current reading is 505, leaving 15 hours. A proposed 18-hour rental would bring the forecast reading to 523, three hours beyond that target. This is a planning exception to resolve before promising the booking, even though the meter has not reached 520 today. Expected use is an estimate; retain the actual checkout and return readings separately.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Three records for one fictional service event</caption><thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>What to retain</th><th>What it does not prove</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Due trigger</td><td>Source reference, next date/meter and review owner</td><td>That the work has happened</td></tr><tr><td>Completed work</td><td>Actual date, reading, provider, work and evidence</td><td>That all release requirements are satisfied</td></tr><tr><td>Availability release</td><td>Documented result, authorized person and usable-from time</td><td>That every future booking fits</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>If a meter is replaced or corrected, keep the old reading and explanation. A smaller reading is not negative use or permission to erase the asset history. Resolve the change using the equipment's records before recalculating a due threshold.</p><p>This resource follows individual rental equipment. It is not a complete vehicle-fleet spreadsheet with fuel, registration or driver records. Use the <a href="/tools/equipment-maintenance-due-planner/">maintenance due planner</a> for entered thresholds, then check the confirmed available pool in the <a href="/tools/rental-booking-conflict-checker/">booking conflict checker</a>. Neither tool determines safe release.</p></section>'''),
'tools/document-renewal-calendar/index.html':('renewal-boundary-review','''<section id="renewal-boundary-review"><h2>Review the start date without losing the original expiry</h2><p>The sample expires on November 15, 2026. Subtracting 45 calendar days produces October 1. At an October 16 review, renewal work is 15 days late to start while expiry is still 30 days away. These are two separate signals; changing the status to Renewal in progress does not extend the expiry or clear the renewal-start date.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Fictional renewal action at the date boundaries</caption><thead><tr><th>Review date</th><th>Start-renewal result</th><th>Record decision</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>October 1</td><td>Due today</td><td>Assign the owner and next action</td></tr><tr><td>October 16</td><td>15 days overdue to start</td><td>Check progress and the remaining source deadline</td></tr><tr><td>After verified replacement</td><td>Old row may be archived</td><td>Retain history; enter a new unique reference and new expiry</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The look-ahead boundary includes the final selected date. With October 16 as the review date and a 30-day window, a renewal-start date of November 15 is inside the window; November 16 is outside it. This counts calendar days, not working days or provider processing days.</p><p>Before archiving, verify the replacement in your real records. Keep its new expiry in a separate active row and use a distinct reference, such as DOC-01-2027. The worksheet excludes archived rows from action totals but does not verify that a replacement exists. Retain original documents securely outside this planner.</p><h3>When a contract needs a different record</h3><p>This calendar covers entered document dates and manual renewal actions. It does not read termination clauses, calculate a verified notice deadline, send alerts or manage approvals. For a contract, record the source clause and confirm the actual required dates separately. The <a href="/journal/contract-renewal-workflow/">contract renewal workflow</a> explains the wider review; check its scope before treating a planning date as a contractual deadline.</p><p>For a handoff, save a dated JSON backup and keep the CSV or PDF report with the review date. Another person can edit a compatible JSON copy, but separate browser copies do not synchronize. Nominate one owner for the current working record.</p></section>'''),
'tools/rental-booking-conflict-checker/index.html':('booking-boundary-review','''<section id="booking-boundary-review"><h2>Check the shared pool and the exact release boundary</h2><p>The capacity input is one pool of interchangeable equipment. Three units of unrelated equipment cannot satisfy a booking for three projectors. Confirm the usable pool first, including maintenance holds recorded outside this worksheet, then enter every relevant reservation for that group.</p><p>With capacity two and zero turnaround, enter three fictional one-unit bookings: A from 09:00 to 11:00, B from 10:00 to 12:00, and C from 10:30 to 11:30, all on the same date in UTC. Each pair needs only two units. Together, however, the bookings need three from 10:30 to 11:00. The conflict is one unit for 30 minutes. Resolve the combined interval rather than accepting the bookings because each pair looked possible.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Back-to-back example for one unit; all times UTC</caption><thead><tr><th>First booking ends</th><th>Turnaround</th><th>Next start</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>12:00</td><td>0 minutes</td><td>12:00</td><td>No overlap at that boundary</td></tr><tr><td>12:00</td><td>30 minutes</td><td>12:15</td><td>15-minute overlap</td></tr><tr><td>12:00</td><td>30 minutes</td><td>12:30</td><td>Released at that exact boundary</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A no-conflict result describes the inputs, not a confirmed return or inspection. If equipment comes back late, update the affected booking window and calculate again. Keep the original promise and the agreed change in your booking record; this checker does not maintain that change history for you.</p><p>Retain the source timezone and confirmed UTC conversion outside the worksheet. Local times can repeat when clocks move back; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/datetime/resolve-ambiguous-times" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Microsoft's explanation of ambiguous times</a> describes why one local clock value may map to two UTC instants. This planner does not resolve that ambiguity or perform a conversion.</p><p>Review the <a href="/journal/rental-equipment-maintenance-log/">equipment maintenance log</a> before deciding available capacity. Property rent, deposits, customer invoicing and equipment release evidence remain separate records. Save one editable backup per resource group so another calculation does not replace your only working copy.</p></section>'''),
'journal/agency-capacity-planning-example/index.html':('capacity-review-handoff','''<section id="capacity-review-handoff"><h2>Include the review work when outside help takes delivery hours</h2><p>The proposed launch takes Leo from 115 to 145 development hours against 128 usable hours. Moving 20 hours to a contractor brings his delivery load to 125, leaving three hours. That is not enough if he must also spend four additional hours reviewing the contractor's work: his revised load becomes 129, one hour over capacity.</p><p>In this separate fictional scenario the four review hours are additional to the original estimates. If they were already included, do not add them again. Record who performs the review, which week it falls in and whether any existing task can move with approval. A contractor's delivery capacity does not create free review capacity in the internal team.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Development role: proposed work and review handoff</caption><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Internal development hours</th><th>Against 128 usable hours</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Existing work plus new launch</td><td>115 + 30 = 145</td><td>17 over</td></tr><tr><td>Move 20 delivery hours outside</td><td>145 − 20 = 125</td><td>3 unassigned</td></tr><tr><td>Add four new review hours</td><td>125 + 4 = 129</td><td>1 over</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Keep the contractor fee and the remaining internal delivery/review cost in the project-cost scenario. Do not count the outsourced hours twice as both full internal delivery and external work. The price and included costs need their own agreed records; these hours alone do not establish a revised margin.</p><h3>Use the format this guide actually provides</h3><p>The tables on this page are an HTML worked example, not a downloadable Excel resource-planning template. Copying a table into a spreadsheet does not add tested formulas or save a complete planning model. The <a href="/tools/freelancer-weekly-capacity-planner/">free weekly capacity planner</a> is a separate browser worksheet for its stated scope, with its own JSON backup and CSV/PDF reports. CSV is not an Excel workbook with formulas and is not an editable application backup.</p><p>At the weekly handoff, retain the period, named role, usable hours, signed work, remaining estimates, review effort and next decision. Save the prior plan before revising it, and compare actual time with the remaining work rather than assuming the original estimate is still correct.</p></section>'''),
'journal/purchase-order-tracker/index.html':('receipt-reconciliation-review','''<section id="receipt-reconciliation-review"><h2>Reconcile each receipt before replacing the cumulative balance</h2><p>The workbook's fictional sample starts at 100 ordered, 55 accepted and five cancelled, leaving 40 open. The worked receipt example later in this guide uses 88 accepted and the same five cancelled, leaving seven open. They are separate snapshots. To move a copy of the workbook from the first snapshot to the second, the net additional accepted quantity is 33, not another 88.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Fictional line reconciliation in the same tracking unit</caption><thead><tr><th>Snapshot</th><th>Ordered</th><th>Cumulative accepted</th><th>Confirmed cancelled</th><th>Open</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Workbook starting example</td><td>100</td><td>55</td><td>5</td><td>40</td></tr><tr><td>After 33 more accepted</td><td>100</td><td>88</td><td>5</td><td>7</td></tr><tr><td>Duplicate posting to investigate</td><td>100</td><td>121</td><td>5</td><td>−26</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Posting the same 33 accepted units twice would produce 121 accepted and a negative open balance of 26. Keep the negative review signal and inspect receipt references; do not hide it with a minimum-zero formula or record a cancellation to force the line closed. The workbook does not identify duplicate delivery notes automatically.</p><p>In the receipt example, seven open consists of five unshipped units plus two rejected units awaiting the agreed replacement. If an accepted unit is later returned, retain the original receipt and a separate correction or return reference. Decide whether the obligation reopens from the agreed outcome before changing the accepted total. A returned-for-credit unit and a unit awaiting replacement need not have the same treatment.</p><p>After editing a disposable copy, check that the open quantity recalculates, save and reopen it, and compare it with the receipt evidence. The <a href="/tools/purchase-order-template/">purchase-order form</a> creates the order document; this register tracks the quantity balance. Neither file automatically updates the other. Keep source order, receipt and amendment references together, and track supplier payments separately.</p></section>''')}
for relative,(sid,section) in sections.items():
 p=ROOT/relative;s=p.read_text();assert f'id="{sid}"' not in s
 if relative.startswith('tools/'):
  marker='<section class="tool-related"'
  if marker in s:s=s.replace(marker,section+marker,1)
  else:s=s.replace('</main>',section+'</main>',1)
 else:s=s.replace('</article>',section+'</article>',1)
 p.write_text(s)
p=ROOT/'journal/purchase-order-tracker/index.html';s=p.read_text().replace('An existing PO still has 24 accepted units due','An existing PO still has 24 units due for acceptance').replace('creates the basis for a new 12-unit request, subject to case packs, minimum order quantity and cash approval.','creates the basis for a replenishment review. If the approved target inventory position is 48 units, a new 12-unit request would restore that target; 12 is not derived from the 40-unit trigger alone. Confirm case packs, minimum order quantity and cash approval before ordering.');p.write_text(s)
p=ROOT/'journal/small-business-balance-sheet-template/index.html';s=p.read_text();assert s.count('<p>Published October 23, 2026</p>')==1;s=s.replace('<p>Published October 23, 2026</p>','',1);p.write_text(s)
print('Five draft sections; PO wording/target corrected; duplicate balance-sheet date removed. Publication dates unchanged.')
